#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# memory_available

#% text
# `memory_available()` gives the number of bytes the running evaluation may still allocate before its memory limit stops it.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The limit is the session's memory budget (a share of the memory the process may use), or the smaller limit of an enclosing `memory_constrained`.

#% item
# Every top-level evaluation starts with the whole budget; the value decreases as the evaluation allocates.

#% item
# A builder can compare the size of its result with `memory_available()` and give `failure(limit, memory, ...)` before allocating anything; the matrix builders `hankel_matrix` and `toeplitz_matrix` do.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
memory_constrained(memory_available() <= 10^6, 10^6)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
memory_available() > 0
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A matrix too large for the memory left is refused before it is built:

#% cell
memory_constrained(hankel_matrix(1000), 10^6)
#% output
#! failure(limit, memory, [1000, 1000])

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `memory_constrained`, `time_constrained`
